00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.530
Jack: So, let’s look at
parallelisation in more detail.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.809
Parallelisation is a method of processing
in which separate parts of an overall task

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:15.079
are broken up and run simultaneously on
multiple CPUs which in turn reduces the

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.090
amount of time involved in processing.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.430
Leah: This also allows large
problems or processes to be divided

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.550
into smaller ones, which can be
run and solved at the same time.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.279
Imagine a road which consists of
one lane, traffic will be slow, and

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.240
any blockages will cause it to stop.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:36.160
Now think about a motorway with 3
lanes, slow traffic can still go slow

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.870
without holding up the faster traffic
in the other lanes and a lot more

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.910
traffic can get to the end of the
road quicker and more efficiently.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.430
Jack: So how does this work in processing?

00:00:47.259 --> 00:00:50.949
Let’s say, for example, that we
have a list variable of length 100

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:54.449
which contains numerical values,
and that we want to perform a

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.500
mathematical operation on each value.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.450
Leah: For arguments sake, let’s say
that each computation will take 1

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:07.620
second and use 1mb of memory, and that
we have 20mb of RAM available to use.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.289
If we do this in a sequential
manner, we will have to wait

00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:14.630
for the previous computation to
finish before the next one starts.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:19.460
This would then take 100 seconds to
complete this operation [number of

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:24.750
operations x length of time to complete
one operation: 100 x 1 second],

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.979
and process at a rate of 1mbps.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.460
Jack: If with the process, there
are no dependencies [for example

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.390
one operation does not depend on the
outcome of a previous] we can use

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.940
parallel processing to perform multiple
operations at once and maximise the

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.810
use of the memory that is available.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:47.859
With 20mb free, this means we can
process 20 x 1mb operations at a time.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.190
Leah: This process will then take
5 seconds to complete [[number of

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:57.050
operations/memory available] x length
of time to complete one operation:

00:01:57.429 --> 00:01:59.500
[100/20 mb] x 1 second] and process
at a rate of 20mbps, therefore 20

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:07.979
times faster than our first example

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.550
However, to ensure that
processes can be parallelized,

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.540
there are certain dependencies.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.929
Jack: This is where pure functions
are useful for parallelisation.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.029
If all of the parallel processes
were dependent on a global variable

00:02:22.059 --> 00:02:25.940
that it modified, like an impure
function could, this would change the

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.989
outcome each time a process completed.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.350
We can therefore use pure functions with
no external dependencies to ensure that a

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.969
parallel process can be computed reliably.

